## Parent
- Spec: benchmark-gepa-overhaul
- Goals: G-1, G-3
- Depends on: bench-result-substrate
- Frozen decisions: F-1

## Problem

Parent goal: Grow the benchmark with FeatureBench, the Gin render-context task, and a few cherry-picked tasks, in a format GEPA can optimize against once-run stored baselines, with a model judge for structural-tool applicability.

This curd ships G-1 (FeatureBench tasks run in our harness) and the external half of G-3 (one Go and one Rust SWE-bench Multilingual instance beside the three local hard tasks), under F-1 (fully native, no Docker). Today the harness can only run tasks whose repository is a pinned fixture: `run_single` copies `get_repo_path(task.repo)` (`benchmark/run.py:152-156`) through `_agent_repo` (`benchmark/run.py:322-369`), `main` exits when a task repo is not cloned under `REPOS` (`benchmark/run.py:1123-1133`) and cleans every selected repo after the run (`benchmark/run.py:1310-1312`), and `Task.source` indexes `REPOS` for every non-synthetic task (`benchmark/tasks/base.py:84-93`). Grading is required strings or one `test_command` (`benchmark/tasks/base.py:143-186`); the only held-out-test grader is the bespoke Gin task (`benchmark/tasks/gin_render_context_tasks.py:116-167`), whose held-out tests sit inside the tilth `benchmark/` tree (`benchmark/tasks/gin_render_context_tasks.py:17`) and whose failure reason carries held-out test output (`benchmark/tasks/gin_render_context_tasks.py:155-156`). The row carries only `correct` and `correctness_reason` from grading (`benchmark/run.py:783-788,848-849`), and every cell gets the same fixed 600 s agent timeout (`benchmark/run.py:685,719`), too short for a Rust compile plus an agent fix. Nothing detects an agent that reads the benchmark tree, where local ground truth lives, or fetches the upstream fix; tilth trusts absolute paths and Bash can read anywhere.

## Contract

The external task adapter (parent curd c2) changes only the benchmark harness.

- **Task hooks.** `run.py` honors three optional hooks on a task, used only when present (F-1):
  - `prepare(workdir)`: when a task defines it, `run_single` creates a fresh temporary workdir and calls the hook instead of copying a `REPOS` fixture through `_agent_repo`, and `main` skips `REPOS` validation and cleaning for that task. Tasks without the hook keep today's `_agent_repo` path unchanged.
  - `grade_details()`: called after `check_correctness`; its dict is merged into the row.
  - `timeout_s`: an optional task attribute, default 600 (today's value). `run.py` passes it to the runner as both the streaming kill timer (`benchmark/run.py:685`) and the non-streaming subprocess timeout (`benchmark/run.py:719`), and the timeout message names it. External Go and Rust tasks set 1800 and FeatureBench Python tasks set 1200 [AGENT-DECIDED; alternative one global `--timeout` flag]. bench-result-substrate already hashes `timeout_s` into the run key, so its contract is unchanged.
- **Harness data directory.** Dataset rows, upstream mirrors, gold patches, held-out tests, FAIL_TO_PASS and PASS_TO_PASS lists, and admission verdicts live only in `$TILTH_BENCH_DATA` (default `~/.local/share/tilth-bench/external`) [AGENT-DECIDED; alternative `/tmp/tilth_bench/external` beside the fixture repos]. It lies outside the tilth checkout and every workdir.
- **Loading (G-1, G-3).** `external.featurebench.load` and `external.swebench_ml.load` build an `ExternalTask` from a dataset row cached in the harness data directory at the pinned revision: FeatureBench Lite dataset v1.1, and SWE-bench Multilingual at HF revision `846e647b`. A load at any other revision raises.
  - The task's `name` is the instance ID, its `repo` is the dataset name (`featurebench` or `swebench_ml`) [AGENT-DECIDED; alternative the upstream `owner/repo` slug], its `source` names the upstream URL and `base_commit` without indexing `REPOS`, and its `prompt` is the problem statement alone.
  - `ExternalTask` exposes `problem_statement: str` and `gold_patch: str` attributes, read verbatim from the row; the parent model judge (c4) labels external tasks from them.
  - `run.py --tasks` resolves an instance ID not in `TASKS` through the cached admission verdicts. `--tasks all` keeps meaning the local registry only [AGENT-DECIDED; alternative a separate `--external` flag].
  - The FeatureBench candidate set is the COSPA `featurebench_lite_pareto12_v1.json` instance list, CPU-only Level 1, minus the parent-deferred heavy-environment repositories (pandas, astropy, transformers, Lightning, mlflow).
  - The SWE-bench Multilingual cherry picks are Go `gin-gonic__gin-3741` with fallback `prometheus__prometheus-14861`, and Rust `sharkdp__bat-2650` with fallback `tokio-rs__tokio-6724`.
- **Prepared workspace (F-1).** `ExternalTask.prepare(workdir)`:
  - exports the `base_commit` tree from the upstream mirror with `git archive` into a fresh `git init` repository whose single commit is that tree, with no upstream history and no remote;
  - for FeatureBench, reconstructs the masked Level 1 state before that commit: it applies the row's own mask or removal patch with `git apply` when the row ships one, else reverse-applies the gold patch, then removes the FAIL_TO_PASS test files [AGENT-DECIDED; alternative reverse-gold only]. The method used (`mask_patch` or `reverse_gold`) is recorded in the task's `source.transformation`, which already lands in every row's `source`, and in the admission verdict `reason` [speculating: either method reproduces the FeatureBench masked state; both are native, and only the admission round trip proves it per task];
  - writes no gold patch, test patch, held-out test file, or FAIL_TO_PASS/PASS_TO_PASS list into the workdir.
- **Native environment (F-1).** Python instances get a `uv` venv at `<workdir>/.venv` built from the instance install settings, reusing the uv cache [AGENT-DECIDED; alternative a shared per-instance venv outside both trees]. Go and Rust instances use the host toolchain and fetch modules or crates with `go mod download` or `cargo fetch` at prepare time. `.venv/` and `target/` go into `.git/info/exclude`. No code path in loading, preparing, admitting, or grading starts `docker` or `podman`.
- **Identity.** An external cell's task digest covers the instance row (gold patch, test patch, test lists, `base_commit`, data revision), and its env fingerprint covers the toolchain version (`uv` and Python, `go`, or `rustc` and `cargo`) and the lockfile hash, so a change in any of them changes the bench-result-substrate run key.
- **Native grading (F-1).** `ExternalTask.check_correctness(result_text, repo_path)`:
  1. extracts the agent's changes against the base commit, ignoring excluded paths;
  2. applies them to a clean prepared checkout in a temporary directory;
  3. resets every file the held-out tests touch to its prepared state and materializes the held-out tests from the harness data directory, so agent edits to those test files do not count;
  4. runs the FAIL_TO_PASS and PASS_TO_PASS tests natively and parses per-test verdicts from pytest `-rA`, `go test -v`, or `cargo test` output; a listed test missing from the output counts as failed. The test command comes from the instance row (`eval_script` for SWE-bench Multilingual, install settings for FeatureBench) [speculating: the command extracts cleanly from those fields; the admission round trip proves it per task].
  - `correct` is true only when every listed test passes. `grade_details()` returns only `pass_rate` (passed over FAIL_TO_PASS plus PASS_TO_PASS) [AGENT-DECIDED; alternative FAIL_TO_PASS only], `f2p_passed`, `f2p_total`, `p2p_passed`, and `p2p_total`.
- **Grader-side material.** The external `correctness_reason` may carry held-out test output, as the Gin grader already does; it is grader-side material that the parent strips before any optimizer-side model sees a row. No other row field this curd adds carries a gold patch, held-out test source, or held-out test output. Agent-produced text, including the tool inputs quoted in `contamination_hits`, is kept verbatim.
- **Admission (F-1, G-1, G-3).** `external.preflight.admit(instance_id)` returns a `PreflightVerdict` exposing `admitted: bool` and `reason: str`.
  - It refuses GPU-flagged rows, Level 2 rows, rows that lack a level or hardware field (`unclassified`), and instances whose environment or prepare step fails, each with a named reason.
  - It then runs a round trip through `prepare` and `check_correctness` on this host. The gold patch must resolve. The empty patch and the tampered patch must not. The tampered patch is the gold patch minus its last source hunk, or, for a single-hunk gold patch, minus that hunk's added lines [AGENT-DECIDED; alternative gold plus deletion of the first added function body]. A passing round trip yields `reason: admitted` for SWE-bench Multilingual and `reason: admitted:mask_patch` or `admitted:reverse_gold` for FeatureBench.
  - This curd owns the verdict cache: each verdict is persisted in the harness data directory keyed by instance ID, data revision, and env fingerprint, and `admit` returns the cached verdict for a matching key without starting any subprocess. The panel curd (c3) only reads verdicts through `admit`. Admission makes no model call and needs no `--max-usd`.
  - `run.py` refuses, before any model call, an external cell whose instance has no admitted verdict under the current env fingerprint, naming the instance and telling the user to rerun preflight.
- **Preflight CLI (G-1, G-3).** `python3 benchmark/external/preflight.py` admits the FeatureBench candidate set and, per language, the primary SWE-bench Multilingual pick, then the fallback only when the primary is refused. It prints each verdict and a summary with `featurebench_admitted`, `go_admitted`, and `rust_admitted`. When `featurebench_admitted` is 0, or no Go or no Rust pick is admitted, it says so by name and exits nonzero. Parent AC-1 then refuses the panel rather than passing vacuously.
- **Contamination (F-1).** Every row `run.py` writes, local or external, carries `contaminated` as a bool and is never written without it.
  - For a cell whose row has a trajectory sidecar, `run.py` calls `external.contamination.scan(sidecar_path, task)` over the tool-call inputs in the sidecar [AGENT-DECIDED: inputs only; alternative also scan tool outputs].
  - A hit is an input that names a path under the tilth `benchmark/` tree (`config.BENCHMARK_DIR` resolved, after `~`, `$HOME`, and `..` normalization) or under the harness data directory; or that fetches the task's upstream repository (a `git clone` or `git fetch` of it, or a URL on its GitHub repository, codeload, or raw host) or the task's own package source (`pip download`, `uv pip download`, or an index install of the package name, a PyPI files URL, a crates.io download, or `go get` or `go mod download` of the module path). A local task's upstream is its `REPOS` URL; synthetic tasks have none.
  - c2 never changes `correct`: every row keeps the grader verdict there. A row with any hit records `contaminated: true` and `contamination_hits`; a row with no hit records `contaminated: false`. Consumers apply the rule: the evolution loop (c5) scores a contaminated rollout incorrect and excludes it from reflection, and `analyze.py` excludes contaminated rows only for panel runs, so ordinary local and OpenCode runs are unaffected [AGENT-DECIDED; alternative override `correct` on panel runs only].
  - A row with no sidecar to scan (an OpenCode cell, or an error, timeout, or invalid-cell row built in `main` at `benchmark/run.py:1256-1305` without one) records `contaminated: true` with an `unscanned` hit, so no consumer treats an unverifiable rollout as clean; panel arms always have sidecars [AGENT-DECIDED; alternative `contaminated: false` plus a separate `contamination_scanned: false` field].
  - A stored row that bench-result-substrate reuses and that lacks the field is scanned from its stored `trajectory_path` before it is written.
- **Fixtures.** The work starts by committing a synthetic external instance under `benchmark/tests/fixtures/external/`: a stdlib-only Python project in a local bare repository, a gold patch, a test patch, FAIL_TO_PASS and PASS_TO_PASS lists, and level and hardware fields, plus a FeatureBench-shaped variant and canned `go test -v` and `cargo test` outputs. Tests make no network or model call.

